From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 15:28:56 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 213/221] feat(area): road function component

"Road" in Add Component -> Area, decision 04 option R1. It binds by rule to the
spline shape on the entity and drapes a road over the terrain along it.

The render nodes are IRoadRenderNodes owned directly by the component, in chunks
of 16 sectors exactly as CRoadObject::UpdateSectors does
(MAX_TRAPEZOIDS_IN_CHUNK, RoadObject.cpp:258), including the 7.5 cm overlap at
each chunk boundary that hides the seam two f16 meshes leave. Each is marked
with SetOwnerEntity, so COctreeNode::SaveObjects skips it and the level export
never contains a road an entity already owns. They are not in entity slots,
because SetVertices takes world vertices and a road is a world-space idea, so
moving the entity rebuilds - which is what CRoadObject::InvalidateTM does too.

The centre line is walked by arc length rather than by the legacy object's
per-segment step counts. The strip is the same shape; evenly spaced sectors are a
small improvement, because legacy rounds its step count per Bezier segment and a
short segment therefore gets a coarser road than a long one. Per-point width
comes from the spline through ISplineShape::GetLocalWidth, which is
CRoadObject::GetLocalWidth's blend.

Properties are the legacy set: Width, Border Width, Step Size, Tile Length, Sort
Priority, View Distance Ratio, Ignore Terrain Holes, Physicalize, plus a Material
that falls back to the entity's.

Interface/IRoadAlign.h declares IRoadAlignSource, the flat POD contract the
editor's Align Terrain To Road action reads the centre line through - terrain
alignment has to live in the editor, because it rewrites CHeightmap and no engine
module can reach that.

